1. Store 1 million user sessions, need to check if a user is already logged in in O(1)

For this scenario, I would use a HashSet to store each user session token. 
I would do this for two reason: one, each element in a HashSet must be
unique, and each session token must also be unique. This works for both practical
and security reason. Two, each lookup (contains check) of a token is an average
of O(1) time complexity. This makes it perfect to quickly and effeciently 
referening a unique session token with all other logged in user to check
if a user is already logged in.


2. Store all events in the order they occurred; frequently add to the back and remove from the front

Since we are frequently adding to the back and removing from the front, that is a 
FIFO (first in first out) collection type, which matches a simple queue. An ArrayList
in Java functions as a queue and is dynamically resizable which I feel would be 
benefitial in for this use case so that is the collection type I would use.

3. Store employee records; need to retrieve by employee ID in O(1) and also iterate in ID order

I would use a LinkedHashMap in this case because it keeps insertion order and has a retieval time
of O(1) which is perfect for this scenario.


4. Store a set of banned IP addresses; need O(1) lookup; no ordering needed

I would use a regular HashSet in this case because it contains only unique elements, and with
banned IP addresses you only need the one unique entry. Additionally, a HashSet has a constant retrieval
time which is effecient and what this specification requires. Lastly, a HashSet does not keep
entries in order, and ordering is not needed for this case so it works out well.
